# Every node of every graph this model composes, with what that graph's nodes carry. One row per
# (graph, node).
#
# A node must be joinable
#   That means the edges must not lose the key first. A node set derived by dedup'ing the
#   endpoints of a flattened edge list is a set of display strings: nothing can be joined to it,
#   so a drawing of the layer graph can carry no fact about a layer except its name, and a
#   renderer with no rank to read draws its own sentinel.
#
# Join at real grain, flatten afterwards
#   The order of operations is the whole point. The layer arm joins `rank/evaluation_order.sqlc`
#   on `(filing, layer)`, which is the conformed key `diagrams/lane_grain.sqlc` asserts is whole,
#   and only then concatenates for the drawing's node id. `rank/graph_edges.sqlc` flattens up
#   front because everything downstream of it counts. Same idiom, opposite order, and the order
#   is what decides whether a fact can still be reached.
#
# `rank` on the unit arm is `notApplicable`
#   It is NULL there, and that is `notApplicable`, not `unmeasured`. Rank is `max(depth)` over
#   `F`, a relation on layers. A unit is not a layer and has no position in that order, so there
#   is no number missing here and none to go looking for. A renderer must draw no label rather
#   than a number, because printing one states an ordinal for a subject the model has no ordinal
#   for, which is worse than printing nothing.
#
# What could refute each layer
#   The second fact a layer node carries is how much of this repository can contradict it.
#   `rank/layer_reach.sqlc` counts the rules that examine each layer, and `violated` is how many
#   of them said no. Joined here, the drawing of `F` stops being a picture of what was filed and
#   becomes a picture of what could be refuted, which is the only thing that separates a diagram
#   somebody believes from one they can argue with.
#
# Joinable only through a key that resolves
#   A rule that declares `layer` and returns a claim address in that column names no layer, and
#   this join then drops those pairs, can shade most of the graph as thinly checked, and is wrong
#   about which. `checks/grain.sqlc`'s `unresolvable` arm is what keeps it true.
#
# Both are NULL on the unit arm, for rank's reason
#   No rule declares a unit as its subject, so a unit is not thinly checked, it is outside the
#   checker's dimension entirely. A zero would be a claim that somebody looked; the absence is
#   that nobody could.
#
# The nodes are the edges' endpoints, not the dimension
#   That is correct, and worth saying. Not every filed layer is in the layer graph: a layer in no
#   part, in either direction, is in no composition and is correctly not a node. Taking the nodes
#   from `layers/every_layer.sqlc` instead would draw a graph that says every layer takes part in
#   a composition, which is the drawing asserting what the model denies.
-- rank/graph_edges.sqlc's endpoints, with evaluation_order and layer_reach joined at (filing, layer).
SELECT 'layers' AS graph,
       e.filing || '/' || e.layer AS node,
       r.rank                     AS rank,
       c.examined_by              AS examined_by,
       c.violated                 AS violated
FROM (
    SELECT p.composition AS filing, p.composed_layer AS layer
    FROM ( :compose(composition/parts.sqlc) ) p
    UNION
    SELECT p.part_filing, p.part_layer
    FROM ( :compose(composition/parts.sqlc) ) p
) e
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(rank/evaluation_order.sqlc)
) r ON r.filing = e.filing AND r.layer = e.layer
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(rank/layer_reach.sqlc)
) c ON c.filing = e.filing AND c.layer = e.layer
UNION ALL
SELECT 'units', u.unit, NULL, NULL, NULL
FROM (
    SELECT c.from_unit AS unit FROM ( :compose(units/conversions.sqlc) ) c
    UNION
    SELECT c.to_unit        FROM ( :compose(units/conversions.sqlc) ) c
) u
